            <div xml:id="d692">

                <pb facs="0656" n="648" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg648"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/7048</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chinese Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hu Shih</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-03-11">March 11, 1940</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I have the honor to
                    inform you that I have received from Generalissimo Chiang Kai-shek the following
                    telegraphic message, dated Chungking, March 9, 1940, for transmission to the
                    President of the United States: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>To the Chinese Government and people who are fighting in resistance to
                            aggression, the newly announced Export-Import Bank credit will mean not
                            only fresh material assistance, but also new moral encouragement. All
                            this has been made possible only by your leadership and direction.
                            Please accept my sincere expression of appreciation and gratitude.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>I shall be greatly obliged if you will be good enough to transmit the above
                    message to the President.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hu Shih</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
